title: Example - Markdown code block - with metadata
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki;vocab=Markdown

The CODE-BLOCK marker has `info-attrs: yes`, so the info string past the first word (language) is parsed as TW macro-parameter attributes. Each parsed `name=value` (or `name:value`) becomes a `data-{name}="value"` attribute on the outer `<pre>` — MkDocs / Hugo / VuePress style. CSS hooks like `attr(data-title)` surface the metadata visibly.

Live:

```python title="src/auth/login.py" highlight="2,5"
def login(email, password):
    return api.post('/login', {'email': email, 'password': password})
```

Source:

````
```python title="src/auth/login.py" highlight="2,5"
def login(email, password):
    return api.post('/login', {'email': email, 'password': password})
```
````

Rendered shape: `<pre class="wltc-md-codeblock wltc" data-title="src/auth/login.py" data-highlight="2,5"><code class="language-python">…</code></pre>`. The title surfaces via CSS in [vocab/markdown/styles](#vocab/markdown/styles):

```css
.wltc-md-codeblock[data-title]::before {
  content: attr(data-title);
  display: block;
  font-size: 0.85em;
  color: #888;
  font-style: italic;
}
```

## Accepted separators and value syntaxes

The parser is TW's `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute`, so all the canonical attribute syntaxes work — `=` and `:` separators, quoted and unquoted values, indirect / filtered / macro values for advanced cases.

Live:

```js author:Alice version=1.2 tags="auth security"
const x = 1;
```

Source:

````
```js author:Alice version=1.2 tags="auth security"
const x = 1;
```
````

Renders with `data-author="Alice" data-version="1.2" data-tags="auth security"` on the outer `<pre>`.

## Security

The `data-` prefix is hardcoded and non-overridable. Anything a user puts in the info string lands as a data-* attribute regardless of name — `onclick="alert(1)"` becomes the inert `data-onclick="alert(1)"`, not a real event handler. URL-bearing attributes like `href`/`src` get the same treatment: `data-href="javascript:..."` doesn't trigger navigation. Future CSS / JS that consumes the data-* attributes still needs to treat the values as untrusted input.

